Listen to that as it truly is, being proclaimed by me, O sinless ones.”
Thus ends the first chapter in the Skandapurāṇa.
6
Sanatkumāra said:
“I take refuge in the god Īśāna, the all-knowing, unconquered / Mahādeva, the great-souled lord of the entire universe,
whose power is unimpeded, and sovereignty in every respect, / whose mastery and omnipresence they declare to be self-accomplished.
Having bowed to that god, accompanied by Umā, self-restrained and pure, / I shall tell the auspicious origin of Skanda to [you who] wish to know the Purāṇic narrative: / the bodily descent of the god Rudra, the Supreme Self; / the consecration of the Prajāpati, and likewise the taking of the head;
the vision of the members of the six lineages, and the releasing of the wheel; / the origin of Naimiṣa, and the completion of the sacrificial session;
and the arrival of Brahmā there, and the practicing of austerity; / the vision of Śarva, and the birth of the Goddess;
and the dispute with Satī, and likewise the curse of Dakṣa; / the birth of Senā, and how the self-choice [marriage] of the Goddess took place;

the granting of boons to the gods, and of the wise Vasiṣṭha; / the birth of Parāśara, and of the great-souled Vyāsa;
the ending of the arising of enmity between Vasiṣṭha and Kauśika; / the desolation of Vārāṇasī, the description of the māhātmya of the sacred field, / and the presence of Rudra there, and the blessing of Nandin;
and the sight of the Gaṇas, and the recounting [of them] in entirety; / the addressing of Kālī, and the practicing of austerity; / the origin of Somanandin, and likewise the granting of boons;
