the becoming Gaurī, the obtaining of a son, and the birth of the Goddess; / Kauśikī’s becoming the mother of spirits, and the lion’s becoming her chariot;
Gaurī’s abode on the Vindhya, the encounter of the Vindhya and the sun; / the greatness of Agastya, the slaying of Sunda and Upasunda;
the destruction of Niśumbha and Śumbha, the slaying of Mahiṣa by her; / and the consecration of Kauśikī […]
[…] and then the granting of boons; / likewise the birth of Andhaka, and the binding of the earth;

and the slaying of Hiraṇyākṣa, and likewise of Hiraṇyakaśipu; / the subduing of the Daitya army, and the vow of the Goddess;
and the departure of the gods, and Agni’s becoming the messenger; / the granting of boons to the gods, and the dismissal of Śukra;
and the origin of the messenger, and the Goddess’s sighting of Andhaka; / the crushing of the wretchedness of Śilāda’s son, and the Goddess’s hundred forms;
the granting of boons by Āryā, and the praise by Śiva and others; / the arrival of the God, and the narration of what transpired;
the story of the devoted wife, and of attendance upon the guru; / the story of Pañcacūḍā, and the unassailability of the fiery one;
the arrival of the messenger, the conversation, then his dismissal; / the conversation with the demon Andhaka, and the arrival at Mandara;”
And the arrival of the gaṇas, as well as the hearing of their enumeration, / and the conflict of Andhaka then in the great battle […],
the granting of half of the body, and the acceptance of the son of Aśoka, / and the origin of Bhasmasoma, as well as the dwelling in the cremation ground,
