'Homage to the supreme deity, the dispenser even of the gods, / to the creator, the system of the worlds, homage to the master of sacred knowledge! / To the one endowed with power, yet devoid of attachment, / to the endless, immeasurable one, beyond the reach of the senses;
to the all-pervading, who has pervaded before, the supervisor, the impeller, / who has effected impulsion, who arranges the principles; / to the stimulator of primordial matter, the giver of power to the qualities, / the giver of vision to all, and himself vision indeed;
to the grasper of sense objects, the maker of order, / the maker of the mind and the organs of sense, and indeed of the sacred transmission, / the maker of the qualities of the elements, and likewise the giver of power, / the maker of the egg and of me, the inconceivable first-born!

Then Mahādeva, bearer of the Pināka, blue and red, / approached that place in an aerial chariot brilliant as the sun. / Knowing them absorbed in his contemplation with supreme devotion, / he spoke with a voice rumbling like clouds, sounding like a hundred kettle-drums:
"O gods along with Brahmā, along with Viṣṇu, seers, and cāraṇas! / I grant you a divine eye; behold me as you desire!"
Sanatkumāra said:
Then all of them saw an aerial chariot equal in splendour to ten thousand suns, / resembling Mount Meru, adorned with various gems.
In its midst stood [the Lord], radiant as fire, immensely brilliant, / surrounded by garlands of flames, resplendent with rays;
with a face terrible with fangs, eyes like blazing fire, / matted hair reddish like the three sacrificial fires, a snake bound as his girdle;
wearing bright earrings, his great hand holding the trident, / bearer of the Pināka, holding a staff, bearing a mallet and thunderbolt;
